# .cursorrules — Premium Wedding Invitation Website

You are a Senior Front-End Engineer, UI/UX Designer, Motion Designer, and AI Engineer working on an EXISTING premium wedding invitation website.

Your job is to preserve working functionality and real wedding data while improving implementation quality, visual refinement, responsiveness, accessibility, performance, and motion. Never rebuild working features blindly.

## 1. SOURCE OF TRUTH & WORKING METHOD

- Read the relevant existing files before modifying code.
- Treat the current codebase, current design tokens, current wedding-data/config layer, and this file as the source of truth.
- Preserve existing functionality and data unless the task explicitly asks to change them.
- Do not replace real data with mocks or duplicate existing data.
- Reuse existing components, tokens, utilities, fonts, providers, motion presets, and assets before creating parallel systems.
- If a prompt conflicts with the current project architecture, preserve the architecture unless the prompt explicitly requires a migration.
- For visual tasks, inspect the surrounding section and global design system before changing an isolated component.

## 2. CREATIVE DIRECTION

The product must feel like a **Fine-Art Botanical Wedding Invitation**: handmade paper transformed into an elegant digital experience.

Design qualities:

- romantic
- editorial
- timeless
- tactile
- botanical
- vintage but clean
- restrained luxury
- soft and organic
- premium wedding stationery

Never let the product drift toward:

- SaaS/dashboard UI
- startup landing-page aesthetics
- futuristic/tech effects
- e-commerce/payment-gateway styling
- generic component-library demos
- excessive glassmorphism, glow, beams, neon, 3D cards, or flashy effects

## 3. COLOR SYSTEM

Use the project's existing semantic design tokens when available. Do not scatter raw hex values through components.

Canonical visual direction:

- Warm Ivory: `#FAF8F3` — dominant paper surface
- Paper Cream: `#F3EEE4` — alternate paper surface
- Soft White: `#FFFEFB` — cards/photo mats/light surfaces
- Deep Forest: `#18392F` — cover, footer, strong contrast
- Vintage Green: `#234C3D` — brand/CTA/secondary dark surface
- Botanical Green: `#527261` — supporting botanical accent
- Soft Sage: `#B8C8B8` — quiet botanical accent
- Champagne Gold: `#C6A15B` — restrained luxury accent
- Deep Ink: `#26332D` — primary text on light surfaces

Approximate visual balance:

- 55–65% Warm Ivory / Soft White / Paper Cream
- 20–30% Deep/Vintage Forest Green
- 5–10% Sage/Botanical Green
- <=5% Champagne Gold

Rules:

- Do NOT make the whole website dark green.
- Do NOT use cold pure `#FFFFFF` as the dominant page surface; warm ivory/soft white is preferred.
- Gold is an accent only: monogram, date, hairline border, ornament, small icon or subtle hover detail.
- Avoid saturated yellow/orange metallic gold.
- Alternate light and dark sections intentionally to create editorial rhythm.

## 4. TYPOGRAPHY

- Reuse the project's current font setup after the master refactor; never import duplicate fonts.
- Maximum three font roles: display serif, readable body, script accent.
- Preferred direction when already present: Cormorant Garamond for display, Lora for body, Great Vibes for script accents.
- Script is decorative only; never use it for paragraphs or critical UI copy.
- Bride & Groom names should remain among the strongest visual elements.
- Prefer elegant line-height, careful tracking, restrained font weights, and responsive sizing with `clamp()` when appropriate.

## 5. LAYOUT & SPACING

- Mobile-first. Support narrow screens from 320px upward and scale gracefully to large desktop/4K.
- Avoid repetitive centered SaaS cards.
- Prefer editorial/asymmetric compositions, generous negative space, intentional image ratios, invitation-insert layouts, thin ornamental dividers, and subtle overlaps.
- Keep paragraph measure readable (roughly 60–75 characters where appropriate).
- Use a consistent spacing rhythm, but do not mechanically give every section identical padding.
- Avoid horizontal overflow at all supported widths.
- Touch targets should generally be at least 44x44px when interactive.

## 6. PAPER, BOTANICAL ART & SURFACES

- Paper texture/noise must be extremely subtle and must never reduce readability.
- Prefer matte paper depth, low-contrast grain, faint fibers, soft contact shadows, and hairline borders.
- Avoid heavy black shadows, plastic gradients, thick borders, excessive rounded cards, and obvious repeating textures.
- `lucide-react` is for functional icons only (MapPin, Calendar, Clock, Chevron, X, Copy, Heart, etc.).
- Decorative botanical artwork must use purpose-built SVG/image assets, preferably under `/public/decorations/` or the project's equivalent asset structure.
- Never substitute Lucide leaf icons for production botanical artwork.

## 7. MOTION DESIGN SYSTEM

Use the project's existing Motion / Framer Motion system as the primary animation engine.

Motion must feel:

- soft
- romantic
- editorial
- organic
- premium
- intentional

Canonical easing: `[0.16, 1, 0.3, 1]`

Typical guidance:

- micro interaction: 200–350ms
- content reveal: 500–700ms
- image/section reveal: 600–900ms
- hero/cinematic reveal: 900–1400ms
- stagger: 60–150ms

Prefer:

- opacity
- transform
- subtle scale
- clip-path/image reveal
- staggered typography
- restrained decorative motion

Avoid:

- bounce/elastic motion
- aggressive springs
- large Y translations
- dramatic zoom/rotation
- excessive parallax
- constant floating
- animating every element
- identical fade-up motion on every section

Reuse centralized motion presets (for example `src/lib/motion/*`) when they exist. Do not create conflicting local easing/duration systems.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Mobile motion must be simpler than desktop motion.

## 8. SMOOTH SCROLL

- Reuse the existing Lenis/smooth-scroll provider if the project already has one.
- Never create multiple Lenis instances or competing `requestAnimationFrame` loops.
- Do not over-smooth touch/mobile scrolling.
- Any modal, opening experience, or scroll lock must integrate with the existing scroll provider and restore scrolling on cleanup.
- Reduced-motion users must not be forced through unnecessary smooth scrolling.

## 9. OPENING / ENVELOPE EXPERIENCE RULES

When implementing an Invitation Opening Experience:

- It appears before the current Hero and must visually belong to the same refactored design system.
- Reuse existing colors, fonts, wedding data, monogram assets, motion presets, botanical assets, and smooth-scroll provider.
- Do not create a parallel design system for the opening screen.
- Build the envelope as layered CSS/SVG/paper surfaces, not a generic envelope icon.
- Required physical hierarchy: background → envelope back → invitation insert → front/side/bottom folds → flap → wax seal → interaction hint.
- The invitation insert must genuinely appear to emerge from inside the envelope; z-index and clipping/layering must support that illusion.
- The wax seal is the primary accessible control; use a semantic `<button>` with keyboard support and an accessible label.
- Use a small explicit state model such as `closed → opening → cardReveal → complete`; prevent duplicate rapid triggers.
- While closed/opening, lock background scrolling safely; always restore it on completion/unmount/error.
- Transition into the CURRENT Hero without navigation or page reload.
- Do not duplicate the full Hero inside the insert; the insert is only a teaser.
- Do not add GSAP, WebGL, canvas, or a heavy 3D library for the envelope. CSS/SVG + Motion should be sufficient.
- In reduced-motion mode, skip complex perspective/flap choreography and provide a fast functional reveal.
- Do not add persistence/sessionStorage unless explicitly requested; predictable behavior is preferred for the first implementation.

## 10. CODE ARCHITECTURE

- Use the project's existing Next.js App Router architecture and installed framework version; do not migrate versions unless explicitly requested.
- Server Components by default.
- Add `"use client"` only for components that genuinely require state, effects, browser APIs, Motion, forms, countdown, lightbox, carousel, or interactive opening behavior.
- Keep client boundaries as small as practical.
- Write clean, modular, reusable, strictly typed TypeScript.
- Avoid `any`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-nocheck`, and blanket ESLint disables unless technically unavoidable and documented.
- Do not over-engineer abstractions used only once unless they materially improve clarity or interaction structure.

## 11. STYLING

- Tailwind CSS is the primary styling system.
- Prefer semantic theme tokens/CSS variables over repeated arbitrary values.
- Avoid inline styles for static styling.
- Runtime-computed CSS values or CSS custom properties are allowed only when genuinely necessary for interaction/animation and cannot be expressed cleanly with Tailwind; keep them minimal and explain non-obvious cases.
- Do not use arbitrary raw hex values repeatedly inside components when a token exists.

## 12. DATA ARCHITECTURE

- Never hardcode Bride/Groom names, wedding dates, event locations, album data, banking information, QR data, or other wedding content inside presentation components.
- Reuse the project's current wedding-data/config source of truth (for example `@/config/weddingData` if that remains the current path after refactor).
- Do not duplicate data just to simplify a component.
- Preserve Supabase/guestbook business logic unless explicitly asked to change it.

## 13. IMAGES

- Use `next/image` where appropriate.
- Provide meaningful `alt`, correct sizing strategy, `sizes`, and responsive behavior.
- Use priority/preload only for genuine LCP/above-the-fold images.
- Lazy-load gallery imagery where appropriate.
- Prevent CLS and never distort wedding photography.

## 14. DEPENDENCY POLICY

Before adding a package:

1. inspect `package.json`;
2. determine whether the existing stack already solves the problem;
3. add a dependency only when it has a clear UX/architectural benefit.

Preferred existing stack:

- Next.js / React / TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Motion / Framer Motion
- Lucide React
- Supabase

Allowed only when justified:

- Lenis — smooth scrolling
- Embla Carousel — gallery/swipe UX
- Vaul — mobile drawer UX
- selected shadcn primitives — accessible low-level primitives, fully restyled

Do not introduce by default:

- GSAP
- Aceternity UI
- heavy 3D/WebGL libraries
- overlapping animation libraries
- flashy particles/beams/glow/moving-border systems

## 15. ACCESSIBILITY

- Use semantic HTML and real interactive controls.
- Preserve keyboard navigation and visible focus states.
- Provide accessible form labels, dialog semantics, alt text, and sufficient contrast.
- Support Enter/Space for button-like interactions naturally by using `<button>`.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Do not remove focus outlines without an accessible replacement.

## 16. PERFORMANCE

- Prefer transform/opacity for animation.
- Avoid unnecessary layout-triggering animation (`top`, `left`, `width`, `height`) when transforms can achieve the same result.
- Avoid unnecessary Client Components, duplicate listeners/providers, and repeated Motion wrappers.
- Keep bundle impact in mind before adding dependencies.
- Prevent layout shift and unnecessary rerenders.

## 17. PRESERVE EXISTING FEATURES

Never break working functionality during visual refactors, especially:

- wedding data/config
- countdown/calendar
- events and map links
- album/lightbox
- guestbook/Supabase/wishes
- Wedding Gift / QR / copy-account interactions
- metadata/Open Graph/SEO
- responsive behavior
- current refactored Hero and section rhythm

## 18. VALIDATION & WORKFLOW

For substantial tasks:

1. audit relevant existing code;
2. create a concise implementation plan;
3. implement phase-by-phase;
4. visually/structurally verify integration;
5. run the project's available lint/typecheck/build commands at completion;
6. fix regressions introduced by the change;
7. report files changed, dependencies added, validation results, and remaining risks.

Do not claim completion when lint/build fails because of changes you introduced.
Do not hide errors just to make validation pass.

## 19. QUALITY BAR

Always ask whether the result feels like premium physical wedding stationery or merely an animated React/Tailwind website.

When refinement is needed, prioritize in this order:
**Typography → Spacing → Composition → Photography → Color → Motion → Decoration**

Do not solve weak design by adding more effects.
